  "sourceQuality": 95,
  "primarySources": 2,
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "text": "When repair ships are busy or far away, a damaged cable stays down longer and traffic runs on thinner backup routes.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "International Telecommunication Union",
      "role": "consequence"
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "text": "A cable ship pays out cable at low speed along a carefully mapped route, and in repairs grapples the cable from the seabed and splices it on deck.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "International Telecommunication Union",
      "role": "mechanism"
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "text": "The key figure: eight knots.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "International Telecommunication Union",
      "role": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "text": "When an ocean cable breaks, who fixes it?",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "text": "A cable ship does, and it lays new ones too.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "text": "It crawls at about eight knots so the cable settles.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "text": "Every metre of the route is mapped as it goes down.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "text": "In 1866, the Great Eastern laid two transatlantic cables.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C9",
      "text": "By 1872, the first ship built just for cable repair appeared.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "text": "So the fastest links on Earth depend on its slowest ships.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "editorial narration (case file)",
      "role": "narration"
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "text": "Cable ships are limited to a speed of eight knots while laying cable so it lies properly on the sea floor.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer"
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "text": "A cable's position must be carefully mapped as it is laid.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer"
    },
    {
      "id": "C13",
      "text": "In 1866 the SS Great Eastern successfully laid two transatlantic cables.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer"
    },
    {
      "id": "C14",
      "text": "Oersted, built in 1872 for the Great Northern Telegraph Company, was the first ship specifically designed for cable repair.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer"
    },
    {
      "id": "C15",
      "text": "USNS Zeus can lay about 1,600 km of cable to a depth of 2,700 m.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer"
    },
    {
      "id": "C16",
      "text": "Cable engines can feed 240 m of cable a minute.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer"
    },
    {
      "id": "C17",
      "text": "Goliath was the first ship to lay an ocean cable, in 1850.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer"
    },
    {
      "id": "C18",
      "text": "Every new or broken submarine cable depends on a cable ship to lay or repair it.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "International Telecommunication Union",
      "role": "dependency"
    },
    {
      "id": "C19",
      "text": "Cable ships are purpose-built, carry specialised handling gear and trained crews, and cannot be improvised from ordinary vessels.",
      "class": "MODEL",
      "layer": "MODEL",
      "source": "International Telecommunication Union",
      "role": "bottleneck"
    },
    {
      "id": "C20",
      "text": "Regional maintenance agreements keep repair ships on standby, and route diversity keeps traffic moving while a repair waits.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "VERIFIED FACT",
      "source": "International Telecommunication Union",
      "role": "resilience"
    },
    {
      "id": "C21",
      "text": "A cable layer or cable ship is a deep-sea vessel designed and used to lay underwater cables for telecommunications, for electric power transmission, military, or other purposes.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "consequence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C22",
      "text": "Cable ships are distinguished by large cable sheaves for guiding cable over bow or stern or both.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C23",
      "text": "Bow sheaves, some very large, were characteristic of all cable ships in the past, but newer ships are tending toward having stern sheaves only, as seen in the photo of CS Cable Innovator at the Port of Astoria on this page.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C24",
      "text": "The names of cable ships are often preceded by \"C.S.\" as in CS Long Lines.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C25",
      "text": "The first transatlantic telegraph cable was laid by cable layers in 1857 to 1858.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C26",
      "text": "It briefly enabled telecommunication between Europe and North America before misuse resulted in failure of the line.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C27",
      "text": "In 1866 the SS Great Eastern successfully laid two transatlantic cables, securing future communication between the continents.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "event",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Lead",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C28",
      "text": "Modern cable ships differ greatly from their predecessors.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C29",
      "text": "There are two main types of cable ships: cable repair ships and cable-laying ships.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C30",
      "text": "Cable repair ships, like the Japanese Tsugaru Maru, tend to be smaller and more maneuverable; they are capable of laying cable, but their primary job is fixing or repairing broken sections of cable.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C31",
      "text": "A cable-laying ship, like CS Long Lines, is designed to lay new cables.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C32",
      "text": "Such ships are bigger than repair ships and less maneuverable; their cable storage drums are also larger and are set in parallel so one drum can feed into another, allowing them to lay cable much faster.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C33",
      "text": "These ships are also generally equipped with a linear cable engine (LCE) that helps them lay cable quickly.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C34",
      "text": "By locating the manufacturing plant near a harbor, cable can be loaded into the ship's hold as it is being manufactured.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C35",
      "text": "The newest design of cable layers, though, is a combination of cable-laying and repair ships.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C36",
      "text": "An example is USNS Zeus (T-ARC-7) the only U.S. naval cable layer-repair ship.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C37",
      "text": "Zeus uses two diesel-electric engines that produce 5,000 horsepower (3,700 kW) each and can carry her up to 15 knots (28 km/h; 17 mph).",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C38",
      "text": "She can lay about 1,000 miles (1,600 km) of telecommunications cable to a depth of 9,000 feet (2,700 m).",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C39",
      "text": "The purpose of Zeus was to be a cable ship that could do anything required of it, so the ship was built to be able to lay and retrieve cable from either the bow or the stern with ease.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C40",
      "text": "This design was similar to that of the first cable ship, Great Eastern.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C41",
      "text": "Zeus was built to be as maneuverable as possible so that it could fulfill both roles: as a cable layer or a cable repair ship.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Modern cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C42",
      "text": "To ensure that cable is laid and retrieved properly, specially designed equipment must be used.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C43",
      "text": "Different equipment is used on cable-laying ships depending on what their job requires.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C44",
      "text": "In order to retrieve damaged or mislaid cable, a grapple system is used to gather cable from the ocean floor.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C45",
      "text": "There are several types of grapples, each with certain advantages or disadvantages.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C46",
      "text": "These grapples are attached to the vessel via a grapple rope, originally a mix of steel and manila lines, but now made from synthetic materials.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C47",
      "text": "This ensures that the line is strong, yet can flex and strain under the weight of the grapple.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C48",
      "text": "The line is pulled up by reversing the Linear Cable Engine used to lay the cable.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C49",
      "text": "The most common laying engine in use is the Linear Cable Engine (LCE).",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C50",
      "text": "The LCE is used to feed the cable down to the ocean floor, but this device can also be reversed and used to bring back up cable needing repair.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C51",
      "text": "These engines can feed 800 feet (240 m) of cable a minute.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C52",
      "text": "Linear Cable Engines are also equipped with a brake system that allows the flow of cable to be controlled or stopped if a problem arises.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C53",
      "text": "A common system used is a fleeting drum, a mechanical drum fitted with eoduldes (raised surfaces on the drum face) that help slow and guide the cable into the LCE.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C54",
      "text": "Cable ships also use “plows” that are suspended under the vessel.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C55",
      "text": "These plows use jets of high-pressure water to bury cable three feet (0.91 m) under the sea floor, which prevents fishing vessels from snagging cables as thrall their nets.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C56",
      "text": "HMTS Monarch (renamed CS Sentinel 13 October 1970) completed the first transatlantic telephone cable, TAT-1, in 1956 from Scotland to Nova Scotia for Britain's General Post Office (GPO).",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C57",
      "text": "The Ocean Marine System Group used a cable laying software designed by Makai Ocean Engineering Inc., in five of their cable installation and repair vessels.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C58",
      "text": "The MakaiLay software has been used by 90% of the worlds' global fleet of cable ships.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C59",
      "text": "These five OMS vessels were installed with this software on August 23, 2023, to reduce failures during installation and increase reliability, safety, speed, and accuracy: CS Cable Vigilance CS Île de Ré (Formerly Alcatel/Alcatel-Lucent/Nokia ship) CS Lodbrog (Formerly Alcatel/Alcatel-Lucent ship) CS Teneo Peter Faber (Formerly Alcatel-Lucent/Nokia ship).",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Equipment",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C60",
      "text": "When coaxial cables were introduced as submarine cables, a new issue with cable-laying was encountered.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Repeaters",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C61",
      "text": "These cables had periodic repeaters inline with the cable and powered through it.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Repeaters",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C62",
      "text": "Repeaters overcame significant transmission problems on submarine cables.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Repeaters",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C63",
      "text": "The difficulty with laying repeaters is that there is a bulge where they are spliced in to the cable and this causes problems passing through the sheave.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Repeaters",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C64",
      "text": "British ships, such as HMTS Monarch and HMTS Alert solved the problem by providing a trough for the repeater to bypass the sheave.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Repeaters",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C65",
      "text": "A rope connected in parallel to the repeater went through the sheave which pulled the cable back in to the sheave after the repeater had passed.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Repeaters",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C66",
      "text": "It was normally necessary for the ship to slow down while the repeater was being laid.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Repeaters",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C67",
      "text": "American ships, for a time, tried using flexible repeaters which passed through the sheave.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Repeaters",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C68",
      "text": "However, by the 1960s they were also using rigid repeaters similar to the British system.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Repeaters",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C69",
      "text": "Another issue with coaxial repeaters is that they are much heavier than the cable.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Repeaters",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C70",
      "text": "To ensure that they sink at the same rate as the cable (which can take some time to reach the bottom) and keep the cable straight, the repeaters are fitted with parachutes.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "Repeaters",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C71",
      "text": "Princess Clementine was a passenger ferry, in 1849, installed two miles (3.2 km) of cable from the coast of Folkestone to the shoreline of railway telegraph lines.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C72",
      "text": "Charles Vincent Walker of the South Eastern Railway Company conceived this cable for to have ship to shore telegraph messages communications.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C73",
      "text": "Goliath, the first ship to lay an ocean cable in 1850.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C74",
      "text": "Done for the Submarine Telegraph Company across the English Channel.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C75",
      "text": "Blazer, the hulk vessel laid the South Foreland and Sangatte cable by Submarine Telegraph Company in 1851.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C76",
      "text": "Red Rover in 1851, a steam tug, worked on the South Foreland and Sangatte replacement cable with new section of armored cable.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C77",
      "text": "HMS Widgeon in 1851, the vessel provided a slice at sea on the new section cable of South Foreland and Sangatte with the failed rendezvous with Red Rover.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C78",
      "text": "CS Monarch (1830–1883), first ship to be permanently fitted out as a cable ship HMS Agamemnon (1852–1870) a converted British warship, involved in the first Ireland and US cabling of 1857.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C79",
      "text": "Cable laying began off Ireland's Ballycarbery Castle in County Kerry.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C80",
      "text": "During this operation, 2,500 nautical miles (4,600 km; 2,900 mi) of cable were stored on each ship, but 300 feet (91 m) of cable was lost over a region known as Telegraph Plateau in the North Atlantic with depths nearly 10,500 ft (3,200 m).",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C81",
      "text": "Attempts with USS Niagara failed twice that year and work was abandoned, until next year, both ships met in the mid-Atlantic for spicing of their cables and HMS Agamemnon traveled east towards Valentia Island.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C82",
      "text": "Additional breakage in the cable occurred and another attempts to lay the cabling were sort of successful.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C83",
      "text": "A failure occurred in the installed cable after a month due to engineer mishandling causing damage.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C84",
      "text": "USS Niagara (1855–1885) a converted American warship, worked with HMS Agamemnon on the first US and Ireland cable run attempt failed route of 1857.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C85",
      "text": "In 1858, another attempt was done with middle Atlantic splicing meet by both ships, and USS Niagara laying cable as traveled westward towards Newfoundland.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C86",
      "text": "Three attempts were done due to further breaks in the cable.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C87",
      "text": "After a month of service, the cable became useless when damage was done by an engineer handling.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
      "id": "C88",
      "text": "SS Great Eastern, worked as a cable ship from 1865 to 1870.",
      "class": "CONFIRMED_FACT",
      "layer": "CONFIRMED FACT",
      "source": "Wikipedia — Cable layer",
      "role": "evidence",
      "verbatim": false,
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cable_layer",
      "section": "List of cable ships",
      "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
    },
    {
